<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panama City</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Panama City, on the Pacific side of the isthmus, was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedrarias-davila">Pedro Arias Dávila</a> in 1519. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> was shipped there and carried across the isthmus to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> fairs, making it a key point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morgan">Henry Morgan</a> sacked it in 1671, and it was rebuilt on a new site. It is now the capital of Panama.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panama-city.jpg" alt="Panama City" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dronepicr · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panama_Papers_(148830809).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 3 November 1903 they declared independence, and the gunboat USS Nashville and the American-run railway prevented Colombian troops landed at Colón from reaching <b>Panama City</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1840 a popular assembly in <b>Panama City</b>, cut off from Bogotá, declared the provinces of Panama and Veraguas independent as the State of the Isthmus, choosing Colonel Tomás Herrera as head of state.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-the-isthmus">Free State of the Isthmus</a></li><li><blockquote>Panama's role was transit: silver from Potosí was shipped to <b>Panama City</b>, carried across the isthmus by mule and river, and traded at the great fairs of Nombre de Dios and later Portobelo for European goods.</blockquote><a href="/state/real-audiencia-of-panama">Real Audiencia of Panama</a></li></ul></section>
